<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macquarie Harbour</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macquarie Harbour is a large, shallow inlet on the remote west coast of Tasmania, entered through a narrow passage known as Hells Gates. From 1822 to 1833 its Sarah Island held one of the harshest penal stations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, for convicts who had reoffended in the colony. The town of Strahan on its shore later served the mining district around Queenstown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macquarie-harbour.jpg" alt="Macquarie Harbour" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M. Murphy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacquarieHarbour.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Macquarie Harbour is a shallow fjord of about 315 square kilometres in the West Coast region of Tasmania, with an average depth of 15 metres and deeper places up to 50 metres; a reported Aboriginal name for it is Parralaongatek. The Gordon River and the King River, which cuts through the West Coast Range, empty into it. So much fresh water pours in through the narrow exit that the harbour has barometric tides, rising with rain in the mountains around it. A man-made rock wall keeps the entrance channel deep and narrow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The harbour was named after Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lachlan-macquarie">Lachlan Macquarie</a>, the fifth governor of New South Wales. James Kelly wrote that he reached it in a five-oared whaleboat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a> on 28 December 1815, but other accounts differ: T. W. Birch told a commission of inquiry in 1820 that Kelly came by boat from Port Davey, and a letter of Birch's dated the discovery to 26 December 1815. Surveyor-General Oxley of New South Wales battled the seas at the heads in March 1820.</p><p>The first settlement was on Sarah Island, named after Birch's wife, chosen for its isolation and harsh climate. The prison held British convicts, among them Irish patriots, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a> were detained there. Later the port of Strahan grew up to serve the mining settlements, chiefly Queenstown, and a short-lived port and townsite at Pillinger in Kelly Basin was built by the North Mount Lyell company. Mine waste from the Mount Lyell Mining and Railway Company polluted the Queen and King rivers and the harbour until the company closed in 1994; an estimated 100 million tonnes of tailings went into the Queen River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Strahan is the departure point for tourism on the west coast, with boats to Hell's Gates, Sarah Island and the lower Gordon River, and the West Coast Wilderness Railway runs along part of the north-eastern shore. Salmon farms, first set up in the 1980s, have brought de-oxygenated zones beneath their pens and concern for the endangered Maugean skate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macquarie Harbour” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The island received around 75,000 convicts, held at stations such as Port Arthur and <b>Macquarie Harbour</b>, and became a disciplined penal society that also exported wool and whale oil.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-tasmania">Colony of Tasmania</a></li></ul></section>
